"A module dependency graph maps source modules and the imports or references that couple them.\n\nUse when: Locate dependency cycles and architectural boundary violations. Estimate the source-level impact of changing a module.\n\nAvoid when: Runtime requests or infrastructure relationships are the concern.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes identify modules at one declared granularity. Directed edges mean imports or references, with type-only imports distinguished when useful. Clusters indicate ownership or package boundaries with an explicit legend.\n\nBuild: Select file, package, or library granularity and record the source revision. Extract or manually verify dependencies using the language’s resolution rules. Collapse irrelevant internals without hiding cycles. Order acyclic regions in layers and route back edges around the perimeter.\n\nCheck: Edge direction consistently points from importer to dependency. Generated, test, and external modules follow a documented inclusion rule. A missing edge is not inferred from incomplete extraction. Cluster titles and node names are clear of dense edge bundles.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing package and file granularity without explaining the hierarchy. Claiming runtime coupling from a type-only dependency.\n\nExample: Checkout imports Pricing and shared Money; a reverse import exposes an unwanted source cycle.\n\nAlternatives: Package diagram, Call graph."
